PATCH 500: 500f walked the DGROUP:0x6d3c cell table with a 4-byte stride and host-deref'd its entries.

asm 0x500f-0x502e (DS=DGROUP=0x1c00):
    5010: bb 3c 6d     mov  bx,0x6d3c
    5013: b9 10 00     mov  cx,0x10
    5016: 8b 07        mov  ax,[bx]        ; WORD entry = an object NEAR OFFSET
    5018: 0b c0        or   ax,ax
    501a: 74 07        je   0x5023
    501c: 8b f8        mov  di,ax
    501e: 83 3d 03     cmpw [di],0x3       ; UNSIGNED WORD compare of the object's type word
    5021: 76 07        jbe  0x502a
    5023: 83 c3 02     add  bx,2           ; WORD stride
    5026: e2 ee        loop 0x5016
    5028: 33 c0        xor  ax,ax
    502a: e8 02 00     call 0x502f         ; AX = the chosen near offset (502f opens `mov di,ax`)

The table is the 32-entry WORD cell index patch 264 already walks in FUN_1000_6049; 500f scans the
first 16 -- the player's own side.  Ghidra typed it `int *`, so `piVar3 + 1` strode FOUR bytes (half the
entries were never examined and each pair was read as one 32-bit value), and then dereferenced the near
offset as a host pointer in `*puVar1 < 4`.

Unreachable until patch 499 unblocked the impact cascade, because 500f only runs from a93e's
`di == word[DGROUP:0x6d34]` branch -- the frame on which the PLAYER object is destroyed.  With 499 in,
AZER1, AZER3, AZER5, AZER6 and CYPRUS1 all SIGSEGV'd here at
  500f <- a93e <- b39c <- c336 <- c31e <- bbb7.
